\title[The Berggren Tree Zeta Function]{The Berggren Tree Zeta Function:\\
Abscissa of Convergence, Counting Law,\\ and the Failure of the Silver-Ratio Heuristic}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{21 August 2026}

\keywords{Pythagorean triples, Berggren tree, Barning--Hall tree, Dirichlet series,
abscissa of convergence, silver ratio, Pell numbers, lattice-point counting,
Tauberian theorem}

\begin{abstract}
The Berggren (Barning--Hall) tree is the rooted ternary tree that generates every primitive
Pythagorean triple exactly once from $(3,4,5)$ by three fixed integer matrices. Its metric
growth is governed by the silver ratio: the dominant eigenvalue of the generators is
$\lam = 3+2\sqrt2 = (1+\sqrt2)^2$, the depth-$k$ layer has exactly $3^k$ nodes, and every
hypotenuse at depth $k$ satisfies $c \le 2\lam^{k+1}$, with equality of exponential order along
the middle spine. We introduce the tree zeta function
$Z(s) = \sum_{w\in\{0,1,2\}^*} c(w)^{-s}$ and determine its abscissa of convergence exactly.
The layer heuristic --- $3^k$ nodes of size at most $\lam^k$ --- predicts the abscissa
$\ssilver = \log 3/\log(3+2\sqrt2) = 0.62324\ldots$. We prove this prediction false: the true
abscissa is exactly $1$. Convergence for $s>1$ follows from a two-dimensional majorant in
Euclid-seed coordinates; divergence for $s\le1$ from embedding the prime seeds $(p,2j)$,
$2j<p$, into the tree and invoking the divergence of $\sum_p 1/p$. For every
$s \in (\ssilver,1]$ the layer majorant converges while $Z(s)$ diverges. We prove the counting
law $H/50 \le N(H) \le 2H$ for $H \ge 512$, so $N(H) = \Theta(H)$, by an elementary
coprimality-and-parity sieve; and a Tauberian bridge showing that the counting law alone forces
$Z(1)=\infty$ with the explicit rate $\hsum(512\cdot128^k) \ge k/300$. Finally we show that both
leg zeta functions have abscissa exactly $1$, via a $\zeta(s)^2$ majorant along two distinct
reindexings of the seed lattice. The structural conclusion is that the layer maximum is a poor
proxy for a typical node: within layer $k$ the hypotenuse ranges from $2k^2+6k+5$ on the slow
spine to $\asymp\lam^k$ on the fast spine. We conclude with the conjecture
$N(H)\sim H/(2\pi)$, supported by six-digit numerics and a Gauss-circle-plus-sieve heuristic,
which would give $Z(s)$ a simple pole at $s=1$ of residue $1/(2\pi)$.
\end{abstract}

